    "snippet": {
      "publishedAt": "2020-03-25T19:08:44.000Z",
      "channelId": "UCorlLn2oZfgOJ-FUcF2eZ1A",
      "title": "React Renderers - Abstracting the DOM - Maël Nison aka @arcanis",
      "description": "Get your ticket to our online workshops here https://www.react-europe.org/#tickets\n\nMaël Nison is a software engineer at https://www.datadoghq.com/ , you can follow him on twitter  https://twitter.com/arcanis\n\nTalk topic: React Renderers - Abstracting the DOM\n\nMost of the React applications we write today use the DOM renderer, generating HTML elements consumed by our browsers. But React is so much more! Its architecture, designed to separate the DOM-specific fragments from the library core, makes it suitable for many other types of applications. In fact, React Native itself leverages these capabilities though its custom renderer that, instead of working with the DOM, manipulates native OS primitives.​ ​But how do those renderers work exactly? Can anyone write one? What makes them a better choice than, say, a regular template language? Are they limited to UI work? We'll discuss all that, and take a quick tour of the existing renderers made those past years.",
